# Linux static check battery

Run from the parent directory of the repos under audit. Adjust `~/Projects` to
wherever the fleet lives. Every command is read-only.

## Syntax: does it parse at all

```bash
# bash
find . -name '*.sh' -not -path '*/.git/*' -not -path '*/venv/*' \
  -exec bash -n {} \; -print

# python
find . -name '*.py' -not -path '*/.git/*' -not -path '*/venv/*' \
  -not -path '*__pycache__*' -exec python3 -m py_compile {} \; -print

# systemd units
find . -name '*.service' -not -path '*/.git/*' \
  -exec systemd-analyze verify {} \;

# node
find . -name '*.js' -not -path '*/.git/*' -not -path '*/node_modules/*' \
  -exec node --check {} \; -print
```

`bash -n` is the highest-yield check in the set. A duplicated keyword or an
unclosed construct reads as plausible to a human reviewer and has never
executed; the script is dead code that looks alive.

`systemd-analyze verify` reports `Command ... is not executable: No such file
or directory` for every unit whose `ExecStart` points at a stale path. That one
message is a whole-repo path-drift audit in a single pass.

## Stale machine-specific paths

```bash
# the previous home directory — substitute the real old username
grep -rn '/home/<olduser>' ~/Projects --include='*' \
  2>/dev/null | grep -v '/.git/' | grep -v '/venv/'

# any absolute home path, to catch variants
grep -rnE '/home/[a-z0-9_-]+/' ~/Projects --include='*.sh' --include='*.service' \
  --include='*.py' --include='*.js' 2>/dev/null \
  | grep -v '/.git/' | grep -v '/venv/'
```

Distinguish config from data: a `reports/*.json` containing an old path is a
historical record, not a bug. A script constant is a bug.

## Tracked-vs-ignored

```bash
git -C <repo> status --porcelain
git -C <repo> ls-files | grep -c '^venv/'   # a committed venv is a finding
git -C <repo> ls-files | grep -c '\.pyc$'   # committed bytecode is a finding
git -C <repo> ls-files | grep -c '^node_modules/'
git -C <repo> ls-files | grep -E '\.env$'
ls -a <repo> | grep gitignore
du -sh <repo>/.git                          # vs working tree
```

Untracked files mean the tool was built but never saved. Read them before
committing: they may be the only copy of working code, and they have never
been through a review or a test run.

**Generated artifacts are findings, and they cluster.** One audit found 1,370
tracked `.pyc` files in a single repo — the same defect it had already fixed
in a sibling repo earlier that session. Fixing one instance proves nothing
about the others, so sweep every repo for the pattern rather than the repo
you happened to be in. Concretely, a tracked `.pyc` is dangerous rather than
merely untidy: it is interpreter-specific and can be imported in preference
to the adjacent source, so the code that runs may not be the code you edited.

Untrack without deleting the working tree copy:

```bash
git rm -r --cached <paths>          # --cached: stop tracking, keep the file
printf '__pycache__/\n*.pyc\n' > .gitignore
```

Distinguish a genuinely committed venv (`git ls-files | grep '^venv/'`) from a
build artifact that merely exists on disk — only the former is a repo-hygiene
finding. A live-refreshed intel/threat cache showing as modified in
`git status` is expected churn, not drift worth committing or reverting.

## Repo vs live system

```bash
# config files with a repo copy
diff -q ~/Projects/x/theme.conf ~/.config/x/theme.conf && echo IN SYNC

# live security config, comments stripped
grep -vE '^\s*#|^\s*$' /etc/security/faillock.conf

# enabled units and their timers
systemctl --user list-unit-files | grep -vE '^UNIT|not-found'
systemctl --user list-timers --all
systemctl --user is-active <unit>
```

## Unit path resolution

`%h` is the home of the user the unit runs **as**:

- user unit (`~/.config/systemd/user/`) → the user
- system unit (`/etc/systemd/system/`) → `/root` unless `User=` is set

A system unit using `%h` without `User=` resolves to `/root/...`, which is a
dead path for every real account. Verify the pairing exists:

```bash
grep -E '^(User=|ExecStart=)' <unit>
```

## Dependency sanity

```bash
# test deps must be declared, or the suite cannot even be collected
cat requirements.txt
grep -l '^import pytest\|^import pytest' tests/*.py
<venv>/bin/python -m pytest tests/ -q
```

A suite whose test modules import a package absent from `requirements.txt`
fails at import time and reports nothing about the code under test.

## GUI app preflight: hybrid GPU + Wayland

Before handing over an install command for anything with a window, check the
three things whose absence produces a silent software-rendering fallback or a
window that never appears. Each reads as "the install is broken" rather than as
the missing dependency it is.

```bash
echo "$XDG_SESSION_TYPE $XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP"    # wayland + KDE?
lspci -k | grep -A2 -iE 'vga|3d|display'        # hybrid? which driver in use
glxinfo -B | grep -E 'OpenGL renderer|direct rendering'
vulkaninfo --summary 2>/dev/null | grep -A4 'GPU id'
```

- **The 32-bit driver stack, not just 64-bit.** Multilib enabled in
  `pacman.conf` is necessary and not sufficient — what 32-bit titles dlopen is
  `/usr/lib32/libGLX_nvidia.so*` plus `lib32-nvidia-utils`. When those are
  missing the app installs cleanly and every title falls back to software
  rendering. Check the files exist, not just the repo section:

  ```bash
  ls /usr/lib32/libGLX_nvidia.so* /usr/lib32/libnvidia-glcore.so* 2>&1
  ```

- **`vulkaninfo` naming `llvmpipe`/lavapipe** means no hardware ICD was
  selected. Each `library_path` in `/usr/share/vulkan/icd.d/*.json` must resolve
  to a real file. A 32-bit Vulkan ICD has no separate package here — it is the
  same `nvidia_icd.json` resolving against the 32-bit `libGLX_nvidia`.
- **`vulkaninfo` loader warnings** (`does not export
  vkGetPhysicalDeviceDisplayPropertiesKHR`) are a known NVIDIA-driver/loader
  mismatch in some builds. Harmless — do not chase it, do not let it delay a
  hand-over.
- **A missing bootstrap/updater popup under Wayland is normal.** The updater
  may run as a detached child that never maps a window, writing progress to a
  log instead. Triage the process table before the display layer.

## Exec, don't infer

```bash
<cli> --help
<daemon>.sh --once            # every daemon should support single-run
systemctl --user start <unit> && systemctl --user is-active <unit>
curl -s -o /dev/null -w 'HTTP %{http_code}\n' http://127.0.0.1:<port>/
```

Use the invocation from the project's own README or `start.sh`. A file run
directly puts its own directory on `sys.path`, not the repo root, so sibling
package imports fail while the file compiles cleanly.
